017_m_66_b038_f001_0971_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_b037_f002_0897_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_b035_f003_0024_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_b037_f002_0829_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_b037_f002_0906_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_b035_f014_051_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_b035_f014_243_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_b035_f014_247_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_b035_f014_252_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_b035_f014_154_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/STL_SPIN_8.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_b035_f014_090_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_b037_f002_2017_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_b035_f014_304_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_b037_f002_1169_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_b037_f002_1122_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_b036_f004_0327_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/b3_jun_6p.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_b035_f003_0069_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_ASR039_366127ds.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/Organ_Front.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g234_0001_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g524_0001_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g526_0001_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2107_m_66_g128_0001_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g525_0001_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g527_0001_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g528_0001_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g151_0001_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g152_0001_pm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g233_0001_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g153_0001_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_b036_f004_0452_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_b036_f004_0455_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_b036_f004_0456_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_b036_f004_0275_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_b036_f004_0447_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_b036_f004_0434_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_b036_f004_0442_mm.jpg)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_100&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_b036_f004_0020_mm.jpg)\n\n- ### Prologue\n\n\n\n\n\n## Introduction\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_1200&f_webp&q_70/images/menu/line.png)\n\n- ### chapter I\n\n\n\n\n\n## Ship in the Box\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_1200&f_webp&q_70/images/menu/line.png)\n\n- ### Chapter II\n\n\n\n\n\n## Sails of Steel\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_1200&f_webp&q_70/images/menu/line.png)\n\n- ### Chapter III\n\n\n\n\n\n## Bouquet of Sound\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_1200&f_webp&q_70/images/menu/line.png)\n\n- ### Conclusion\n\n\n\n\n\n## A Place to Gather\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_1200&f_webp&q_70/images/menu/line.png)\n\n- ### Highlights\n\n\n\n\n\n## Index\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_1200&f_webp&q_70/images/menu/line.png)\n\n\n[Click to open accessible version](https://gehry.getty.edu/accessibility)\n\n[\\[\\\\\nA\\\\\n\\\\\nc\\\\\n\\\\\nc\\\\\n\\\\\ne\\\\\n\\\\\ns\\\\\n\\\\\ns\\\\\n\\\\\ni\\\\\n\\\\\nb\\\\\n\\\\\nl\\\\\n\\\\\ne\\\\\n\\\\\nv\\\\\n\\\\\ne\\\\\n\\\\\nr\\\\\n\\\\\ns\\\\\n\\\\\ni\\\\\n\\\\\no\\\\\n\\\\\nn\\\\\n\\]](https://gehry.getty.edu/accessibility)\n\nE\n\nn\n\ng\n\nl\n\ni\n\ns\n\nh\n\nE\n\ns\n\np\n\na\n\nñ\n\no\n\nl\n\n\\[\\\nA\\\n\\\nc\\\n\\\nk\\\n\\\nn\\\n\\\no\\\n\\\nw\\\n\\\nl\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\\nd\\\n\\\ng\\\n\\\nm\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\\nn\\\n\\\nt\\\n\\\ns\\\n\\]\nS\n\no\n\nu\n\nn\n\nd\n\no\n\nn\n\no\n\nf\n\nf\n\nDvořák: Symphony No.7 in D Minor, Op. 70, B.141 - III. Scherzo\n\n\\[\\\nM\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\\nn\\\n\\\nu\\\n\\]\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/images/intro/sketches/0.8x/center-1.webp)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/images/intro/sketches/0.8x/center-2.webp)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/images/intro/sketches/0.8x/center-3.webp)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/images/intro/sketches/0.8x/center-4.webp)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/images/intro/sketches/0.8x/left-1.webp)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/images/intro/sketches/0.8x/left-2.webp)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/images/intro/sketches/0.8x/left-3.webp)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/images/intro/sketches/0.8x/left-4.webp)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/images/intro/sketches/0.8x/left-5.webp)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/images/intro/sketches/0.8x/left-6.webp)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/images/intro/sketches/0.8x/left-7.webp)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/images/intro/sketches/0.8x/right-1.webp)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/images/intro/sketches/0.8x/right-2.webp)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/images/intro/sketches/0.8x/right-3.webp)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/images/intro/sketches/0.8x/top-1.webp)\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/images/intro/sketches/0.8x/top-2.webp)\n\n\\[Scroll for story\\]\n\nHow Frank Gehry and his team designed a\n\nmonument to music — the story of the Walt\n\nDisney Concert Hall\n\nFrank Gehry's acclaimed Walt Disney\n\nConcert Hall has been called a “living\n\nroom for Los Angeles.”\n\nThis remarkable work of public\n\narchitecture is the result of a decades-long\n\nprocess of collaboration, negotiation, and\n\ninvention.\n\nDuring this time, Gehry and his\n\ncollaborators developed hundreds of\n\nphysical and digital models that pushed\n\nthe limits of technical possibility.\n\nDesigned from the inside out, the Hall\n\nwas conceived with music at its center —\n\na space for music to be heard, seen, and\n\nfelt.\n\nThis is the story of the Walt Disney\n\nConcert Hall, as told through the Frank\n\nGehry archive at Getty.\n\n\\[\\\nC\\\n\\\nl\\\n\\\no\\\n\\\ns\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\]\n\n## You’re leaving the Chapter I interactive story. Scroll up or click to resume.\n\n\\[\\\nR\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\\ns\\\n\\\nu\\\n\\\nm\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\\ns\\\n\\\nt\\\n\\\no\\\n\\\nr\\\n\\\ny\\\n\\]\n\nPrev\n\nThe Interior\n\nEarly in the design process, Frank Gehry and his team identified this scheme, the modified shoebox, as ideal for the Concert Hall’s interior.\n\nFrank Gehry, architect\n\nThis is pretty close to — what, the final?\n\n\\[\\\nE\\\n\\\nx\\\n\\\np\\\n\\\nl\\\n\\\no\\\n\\\nr\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\\nt\\\n\\\nh\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\\nm\\\n\\\no\\\n\\\nd\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\\nl\\\n\\]\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_1200&f_webp&q_70/storyblok/cache/assets/shoebox_thumb-10873812.jpg)\n\n\\[\\\nB\\\n\\\na\\\n\\\nc\\\n\\\nk\\\n\\\nt\\\n\\\no\\\n\\\ns\\\n\\\nt\\\n\\\no\\\n\\\nr\\\n\\\ny\\\n\\]\n\n## Designing a Space for Music\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_2000&f_webp&q_70/images/chapter-1/title/top.png)![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_2000&f_webp&q_70/images/chapter-1/title/sides.png)![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_2000&f_webp&q_70/images/chapter-1/title/bottom.png)\n\n# Sailing the sea of sound\n\nGehry's design approach aimed at once to subvert public perception of his unconventional design methods and to give rise to a concert hall that was appropriately and uniquely “LA.”\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_2000&f_webp&q_70/storyblok/cache/assets/1-01-2023_03_31_wdch2324_brollreel_final_1920x1080_archive_cropped-10566186.jpg)\n\n### Photo by Adam Latham courtesy of LA Phil\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/svg/letters/l.svg)\n\nate in 1988, four designs submitted to the competition for a new concert hall in downtown Los Angeles were first unveiled to the public. Directly adjacent to the Music Center, the proposed Walt Disney Concert Hall was made possible by a $50 million gift by Lillian Disney in honor of her late husband, and the future hall was intended to serve as a new dedicated home for the LA Phil.\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_1200&f_webp&q_70/storyblok/cache/assets/1-02-gri_2017_m_66_b035_f015_261_mm_cropped-10566201.jpg)\n\n### Perspective Rendering for Frank Gehry's entry to the Walt Disney Concert Hall Competition\n\nOf the four finalists in the competition, Frank Gehry’s firm seemed to many to be an outlier: although his team had been involved in increasingly large-scale public and cultural projects by this point, Gehry was still largely known for his work with bold forms and experimental, though commonplace materials including chain-link fencing, stucco, cardboard, and unpainted plywood, none of which seemed appropriate to the design of a civic monument like a concert hall. Gehry’s office, however, devised schemes for the Concert Hall which managed to strike a careful balance: refined, yet informal, serious, yet inviting, the design was deemed befitting of a concert hall which would suit the unique cultural landscape of Los Angeles, and a few weeks later in 1988 it was announced as the competition’s winning entry.\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_1200&f_webp&q_70/storyblok/cache/assets/1-03-job21783_competitiondetails_001_cropped-10566208.jpg)\n\n### Model for Frank Gehry's entry to the Walt Disney Concert Hall Competition\n\n\\[\\\nR\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\\na\\\n\\\nd\\\n\\\nM\\\n\\\no\\\n\\\nr\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\]\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_2000&f_webp&q_70/storyblok/cache/assets/2-09-dch_v1_resized-10634197.jpg)\n\n\\[\\\nC\\\n\\\nl\\\n\\\no\\\n\\\ns\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\]\n\n## You’re leaving the Chapter II interactive story. Scroll up or click to resume.\n\n\\[\\\nR\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\\ns\\\n\\\nu\\\n\\\nm\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\\ns\\\n\\\nt\\\n\\\no\\\n\\\nr\\\n\\\ny\\\n\\]\n\nPrev\n\nThe Exterior\n\nSurrounding the concert hall shoebox, smaller blocks were added in order to understand how the building’s necessary “programs”—lobbies, bathrooms, circulation, and so on—could fit together in a unified scheme.\n\nFrank Gehry, architect\n\n\\[\\\nE\\\n\\\nx\\\n\\\np\\\n\\\nl\\\n\\\no\\\n\\\nr\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\\nt\\\n\\\nh\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\\nm\\\n\\\no\\\n\\\nd\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\\nl\\\n\\]\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_1200&f_webp&q_70/storyblok/cache/assets/formstudy_thumb-10873813.jpg)\n\n\\[\\\nB\\\n\\\na\\\n\\\nc\\\n\\\nk\\\n\\\nt\\\n\\\no\\\n\\\ns\\\n\\\nt\\\n\\\no\\\n\\\nr\\\n\\\ny\\\n\\]\n\n## A Once-Impossible Build\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_2000&f_webp&q_70/images/chapter-3/title/top.png)![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_2000&f_webp&q_70/images/chapter-3/title/bottom.png)![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_2000&f_webp&q_70/images/chapter-3/title/center.png)![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_2000&f_webp&q_70/images/chapter-3/title/left.png)\n\n# Shaping curves\n\nFrank Gehry and his team engaged in an unprecedented traffic between handcraft and digital tooling in developing designs for the Walt Disney Concert Hall.\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_2000&f_webp&q_70/storyblok/cache/assets/3-01-2010_wdch_ext_sunrise_al_1_resized-10567181.jpg)\n\n### Photo by Adam Latham courtesy of LA Phil\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/svg/letters/a.svg)\n\nt the project's inception, the building’s exterior form was to be worked out through an iterative process of constructing models by hand. With the concert hall shoebox at the center, the building’s various programmatic requirements were fleshed out with hand-cut elements in wood representing stairs, elevators, mechanical systems, offices, and other “secondary” spaces.\nPlaced on the site, these various elements together suggested how the concert hall’s monumental form could be broken down to meet the urban context at a more human scale.\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_1200&f_webp&q_70/storyblok/cache/assets/3-02-gri_2017_m_66_b038_f001_0538_mm_resized-10567221.jpg)\n\n### Constructing an Exterior Model in Paper and Acrylic\n\n## Frank Gehry’s team next turned to paper models to understand how these various programmatic and scalar needs could be enwrapped in a single architectural gesture. Initially envisioned as a dynamic form clad in limestone, the building's exterior was later changed to stainless steel—largely owing to aesthetic and budgetary considerations.\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_1200&f_webp&q_70/storyblok/cache/assets/3-03-gri_2017_m_66_b038_f001_2190_r_mm_resized-10567248.jpg)\n\n### Constructing an Exterior Model in Paper and Acrylic\n\n## handcrafted possibilities\n\nTape, glue, and strips of paper reveal an ongoing process of adjusting the building’s form, approximating complex curvatures, and considering engineering opportunities and constraints.\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_1200&f_webp&q_70/storyblok/cache/assets/3-04-gri_2017_m_66_b038_f001_0337_mm_resized-10567245.jpg)\n\n### Exterior Study Model\n\n\\[\\\nR\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\\na\\\n\\\nd\\\n\\\nM\\\n\\\no\\\n\\\nr\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\]\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_2000&f_webp&q_70/storyblok/cache/assets/1-26-wdch_interiorapril11-0035_cropped-10566631.jpg)\n\n\\[\\\nC\\\n\\\nl\\\n\\\no\\\n\\\ns\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\]\n\n## You’re leaving the Chapter III interactive story. Scroll up or click to resume.\n\n\\[\\\nR\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\\ns\\\n\\\nu\\\n\\\nm\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\\ns\\\n\\\nt\\\n\\\no\\\n\\\nr\\\n\\\ny\\\n\\]\n\nPrev\n\nThe Organ\n\nCaptured here is the team's effort to develop a sculpturally dynamic, yet fully functional organ. Handwritten notes atop each pipe indicate that none is merely decorative.\n\nFrank Gehry, architect\n\n\\[\\\nE\\\n\\\nx\\\n\\\np\\\n\\\nl\\\n\\\no\\\n\\\nr\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\\nt\\\n\\\nh\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\\nm\\\n\\\no\\\n\\\nd\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\\nl\\\n\\]\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_1200&f_webp&q_70/storyblok/cache/assets/organ_thumb-10873811.jpg)\n\n\\[\\\nB\\\n\\\na\\\n\\\nc\\\n\\\nk\\\n\\\nt\\\n\\\no\\\n\\\ns\\\n\\\nt\\\n\\\no\\\n\\\nr\\\n\\\ny\\\n\\]\n\n## Reinventing the Organ\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_2000&f_webp&q_70/images/chapter-2/title/main.png)![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_2000&f_webp&q_70/images/chapter-2/title/01.png)![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_2000&f_webp&q_70/images/chapter-2/title/02.png)![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_2000&f_webp&q_70/images/chapter-2/title/03.png)![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_2000&f_webp&q_70/images/chapter-2/title/04.png)\n\n# Making noise\n\nThe organ, a sculptural ensemble of pipes in metal and in Norwegian wood, crowns the symmetrical interior of the Walt Disney Concert Hall. Inaugurated a full year after the Hall first opened, the organ represented a significant, cross-disciplinary achievement whose complex design, construction, and sound testing required additional time, negotiation, care, and commitment to realize.\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_2000&f_webp&q_70/storyblok/cache/assets/2-01-wdch_interiorapril11-0116_cropped-10566763.jpg)\n\n### Photo by Adam Latham courtesy of LA Phil\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/svg/letters/c.svg)\n\nollaborating with Manuel Rosales Jr., a renowned organ designer and builder, Frank Gehry spent nearly three years heavily involved in the design of the organ. During this period, the architect’s office produced numerous variations across more than forty models. This process took what Gehry recalled as a “circuitous” journey, before the design was ready to be delivered to Glatter-Götz Organ Builders, a German organ-building company. The result: a remarkably unique organ, exceptional both as an instrument to be heard and as a work to be seen.\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_1200&f_webp&q_70/storyblok/cache/assets/2-02-9c4u1766_cropped-10566768.jpg)\n\n### Manuel Rosales Jr. working at the organ Photo by Laurence Bartone\n\n## “Among his many accomplishments, Frank Gehry will be remembered as the architect who elevated the organ to be the magnificent centerpiece of his design for the Walt Disney Concert Hall.”\n\n### Manuel rosales jr., organ builder\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_1200&f_webp&q_70/storyblok/cache/assets/2-03-wdch_interiorapril11-0086_cropped-10566783.jpg)\n\n### The many pipes which comprise the organ Photo by Adam Latham courtesy of LA Phil\n\n## Why an organ?\n\nWhy an organ of this magnitude, with its 6,134 pipes towering above the concert hall's stage?\nFrank Gehry explains this process as a crescendo of ideas. When, at an early stage, he visited Hans Scharoun's Berlin Philharmonie, Gehry found himself in awe of Scharoun's organ for the immediate power and dynamic presence it afforded the hall. The organ's rich materiality and Baroque sculptural quality, accentuated by the play of light within its immediate surrounds, strongly appealed to Gehry's vision for the Walt Disney Concert Hall. However, he and the project team sensed that the organ's off-axis placement in the Berlin Philharmonie was acoustically less than ideal, and Gehry sought to devise alternate design solutions in his own scheme for the Walt Disney Concert Hall.\nThe setting of the organ on the hall's axis of symmetry thus became essential, as was the creation of an illumination device through which rays of light could bathe the instrument in warm tones from high above. With its playfully curving pipes on its façade—pipes which are in fact functional—the organ embraces the orchestra and audience alike, alluding to the playful dynamism and spirit of invention that Gehry first noticed and admired in Scharoun's organ.\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_1200&f_webp&q_70/storyblok/cache/assets/2-04-2011_wdch_int_auditorium_al_2_cropped-10566773.jpg)\n\n### The Organ in the Hall Photo by Adam Latham courtesy of LA Phil\n\n\\[\\\nR\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\\na\\\n\\\nd\\\n\\\nM\\\n\\\no\\\n\\\nr\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\]\n\n![preview](https://gehry.getty.edu/images/outro/outro-poster-0.jpg)\n\nThe Walt Disney Concert Hall stands today\n\nas a testament to the intensive, collaborative\n\nprocess led by Frank Gehry and his studio — a\n\nprocess which unfolded across media, across\n\ndisciplines, and across many years.\n\n![preview](https://gehry.getty.edu/images/outro/outro-poster-1.jpg)\n\nAs if a musical instrument, the\n\nWalt Disney Concert Hall ushers\n\naudiences into the shared, communal\n\nexperience of hearing music together.\n\n![preview](https://gehry.getty.edu/images/outro/outro-poster-2.jpg)\n\nIf architecture, according to Frank\n\nGehry, is the practice of creating feeling from\n\ninert materials, music suffuses this space with\n\nfeeling of its own — one performance at a time.\n\n# Index\n\n171 Highlights\n\n171 Collection Highlights\n\n1988–2003\n\nGetty Research Institute\n\nHundreds of thousands of sketches, drawings, physical and digital models, photographic images, documentation, and various ephemera spanning nearly 300 projects comprise the Frank O. Gehry papers held at Getty Research Institute. To develop this story, Getty selected 171 highlights from our archive and augmented these with research, interviews, media capture, and 3D modeling work.\n\nTogether, these efforts provide a small, but captivating glimpse into the immense work that went into the design of the Walt Disney Concert Hall (WDCH). Getty’s archival selections are featured below in order of appearance.\n\n[\\[\\\\\nV\\\\\n\\\\\ni\\\\\n\\\\\ne\\\\\n\\\\\nw\\\\\n\\\\\nf\\\\\n\\\\\nu\\\\\n\\\\\nl\\\\\n\\\\\nl\\\\\n\\\\\nc\\\\\n\\\\\no\\\\\n\\\\\nl\\\\\n\\\\\nl\\\\\n\\\\\ne\\\\\n\\\\\nc\\\\\n\\\\\nt\\\\\n\\\\\ni\\\\\n\\\\\no\\\\\n\\\\\nn\\\\\n\\]](http://primo.getty.edu/GRI:GETTY_ALMA21183099180001551)\n\nThe design process for the Walt Disney Concert Hall unfolded across countless models, both physical and digital, and numerous sketches.\n\n\\[01\\]\n\n### Sketch of WDCH Exterior\n\n![A sketch of the exterior of Walt Disney Concert Hall expressively rendered in fine black lines](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_400&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_ASR036_366124ds.jpg)\n\n\\[02\\]\n\n### Sketch of WDCH Exterior\n\n![A sketch of the exterior of Walt Disney Concert Hall and neighboring buildings, rendered in bold black lines](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_400&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_ASR042_366130ds.jpg)\n\n## CHAPTER I  Ship in the Box\n\nAt the building's literal and conceptual center is the concert hall itself, an interior space whose development in many ways directed the overall scheme for the building.\n\n\\[03\\]\n\n### Plan Sketch of WDCH Interior\n\n![A top-down sketch showing the plan for Walt Disney Concert Hall rendered in expressive black lines](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_400&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_ASR043_366131ds.jpg)\n\n\\[04\\]\n\n### Study Model of WDCH Interior\n\n![A model of the Walt Disney Concert Hall interior constructed in simple materials schematically illustrates the dimensions of the rectangular room and the arrangement of seating and the stage within it. Printed lines suggest the placement of aisles, stairs, and other details.](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_400&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_M_66_17_res084_740a_02_373246ds.jpg)\n\n\\[05\\]\n\n### Study Model for WDCH Interior\n\n![A model of the Walt Disney Concert Hall interior constructed in simple materials schematically illustrates the dimensions of the room and the arrangement of seats and the stage within it. Printed lines suggest the placement of aisles, stairs, and other details.](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_400&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g183_0009.jpg)\n\n\\[06\\]\n\n### Study Model for WDCH Interior\n\n![A model of the Walt Disney Concert Hall interior constructed in simple materials schematically illustrates the starburst-like shape of the room and the arrangement of seats and the stage within it.](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_400&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g184_0009.jpg)\n\n\\[07\\]\n\n### Study Model for WDCH Interior\n\n![A model of the Walt Disney Concert Hall interior constructed in simple materials schematically illustrates the dimensions of the mostly rectangular room, with curving forms suggesting balconies for seating.](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_400&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g185_0009.jpg)\n\n\\[08\\]\n\n### Study Model for WDCH Interior\n\n![A model of the Walt Disney Concert Hall interior constructed in simple, beige materials schematically illustrates the dimensions of the rectangular room and the arrangement of seats and the stage. Printed lines suggest the placement of aisles, stairs, and other details.](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_400&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g186_0009.jpg)\n\n\\[09\\]\n\n### Study Model for WDCH Interior\n\n![A model of the Walt Disney Concert Hall interior constructed in simple, beige materials schematically illustrates the dimensions of the rectangular room and the arrangement of seats and the stage within it.](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_400&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g188_0009.jpg)\n\n\\[10\\]\n\n### Study Model for WDCH Interior\n\n![A model of the Walt Disney Concert Hall interior constructed in simple, beige materials schematically illustrates the curving walls of the room and the placement of the stage within it.](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_400&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g190_0009.jpg)\n\n\\[11\\]\n\n### Study Model for WDCH Interior\n\n![A model of the Walt Disney Concert Hall interior constructed in simple, beige materials schematically illustrates the undulating walls of the room and its complex ceiling profile.](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_400&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g198_0009.jpg)\n\n\\[12\\]\n\n### Study Model for WDCH Interior\n\n![A model of the Walt Disney Concert Hall interior constructed in simple, beige materials schematically illustrates the dimensions of the room and the placement of seating balconies and the stage within it.](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_400&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g199_0009.jpg)\n\n\\[13\\]\n\n### Study Model for WDCH Interior\n\n![A model of the Walt Disney Concert Hall interior constructed in simple, beige materials schematically illustrates the undulating walls of the room and the arrangement of seats, balconies, and the stage within it. Printed lines suggest the placement of aisles, stairs, and other details.](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_400&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g221_0009.jpg)\n\n\\[14\\]\n\n### Ceiling Study Model for WDCH Interior\n\n![A study model for the ceiling of the Walt Disney Concert Hall constructed in simple materials suggests complex curving forms contained within a rectangular footprint.](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_400&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g224_0009.jpg)\n\n\\[15\\]\n\n### Ceiling Study Model for WDCH Interior\n\n![A study model for the ceiling of the Walt Disney Concert Hall constructed in simple materials suggests complex angular forms contained within a roughly rectangular footprint.](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_400&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g226_0009.jpg)\n\n\\[16\\]\n\n### Study Model for WDCH Interior\n\n![A model of the Walt Disney Concert Hall interior constructed in simple, beige materials schematically illustrates the alternating curved and straight walls of the room and the arrangement of seats, balconies, and the stage within it. Penciled and printed lines suggest the placement of aisles, stairs, and other details.](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_400&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g405_0009.jpg)\n\n\\[17\\]\n\n### Study Model for WDCH Interior\n\n![A model of the Walt Disney Concert Hall interior constructed in simple, beige materials schematically illustrates the angular walls of the asymmetrical room and the arrangement of seats, balconies, and the stage within it. Printed lines suggest the placement of aisles, stairs, and other details.](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_400&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g406_0009.jpg)\n\n\\[18\\]\n\n### Study Model for WDCH Interior\n\n![A model of the Walt Disney Concert Hall interior constructed in simple, beige materials schematically illustrates the roughly hexagonal shape of the room and the arrangement of seats, balconies, and the stage within it. Printed lines suggest the placement of aisles, stairs, and other details.](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_400&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g407_0009.jpg)\n\n\\[19\\]\n\n### Study Model for WDCH Interior\n\n![A model of the Walt Disney Concert Hall interior constructed in simple, beige materials schematically illustrates the alternating curved and straight walls of the room and the placement of seating balconies within it.](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_400&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g408_0009.jpg)\n\n\\[20\\]\n\n### Study Model for WDCH Interior\n\n![A model of the Walt Disney Concert Hall interior constructed in simple materials schematically illustrates the undulating walls of the room. Half of the model is covered by a ceiling which traces the same outline.](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_400&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g409_0009.jpg)\n\n\\[21\\]\n\n### Study Model for WDCH Interior\n\n![A model of the Walt Disney Concert Hall interior constructed in simple, beige materials schematically illustrates the alternating curved and straight walls of the room.](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_400&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g410_0009.jpg)\n\n\\[22\\]\n\n### Study Model for WDCH Interior\n\n![A model of the Walt Disney Concert Hall interior constructed in simple, beige materials schematically illustrates the angular dimensions of the room and the placement of seating balconies and the stage within it.](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_400&f_webp&q_70/images/archive/gri_2017_m_66_g411_0009.jpg)\n\n\\[\\\nS\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\\nm\\\n\\\no\\\n\\\nr\\\n\\\ne\\\n\\]\n\n![](https://gehry.getty.edu/_ipx/w_2000&f_webp&q_70/images/footer/scribble.jpg)\n\n# Acknowledgments\n\n## Getty developed this digital exhibition through archival research, conversations with Frank Gehry and his many collaborators, and new scholarship, including 3D modeling and analysis.\n\n### By\n\nMaristella Casciato, Serena Parr Cline,\nGary Riichirō Fox, Linda Han, Emily Pugh, Todd Swanson, Ian Webb\n\n## Special thanks\n\nFrank Gehry, Chief of Staff and partner Meaghan Lloyd, the partners of Gehry Partners, LLP, and Megan Meulemans\n\nMotoo Komoda of Nagata Acoustics\n\nLA PHIL AND TEAM : Scott Arenstein, Michele Beacham, Nora Brady, Jessie Farber, Kristen Flock-Ritchie, Raymond Horwitz, Chad Smith, Daniel Song, Natalie Suarez, Meghan Umber, Michael Vitale, Kelvin Vu, Julia Ward\n\n### Web Design, 